{
 "experiment": "E-INC-2b",
 "question": "does material planted late (cycle 3 instead of the opening turn) survive better across the remaining compactions?",
 "plantCycle": 3,
 "answer": "No: late constraints survive WORSE (4/8 and 3/8 retained vs 8/8 and 7/8 when the same lines open the session). Two pipeline mechanisms cause it; both verified against the rendered ledger.",
 "findings": [
  {
   "id": "W9-F1",
   "title": "keyword-less constraint lines are invisible to the ledger when isolated",
   "evidence": "In the baseline all constraints share ONE user message with the line 'never drop ...' (SUPERSEDE_RE matches 'drop'), so is_correction=true and every clause of that message is captured. Planted as separate entries, 'no new dependencies ...', 'preserve ... audit trail verbatim' and 'stays single-writer' match neither CONSTRAINT_RE nor SUPERSEDE_RE and are never captured."
  },
  {
   "id": "W9-F2",
   "title": "retired-id collision silently deletes newly captured items (auto-renumbered ids are not stable across merges)",
   "evidence": "build_ledger assigns ids by insertion count (format!(\"C{}\", constraints.len()+1)) and ends with ledger.constraints.retain(|item| !retired.contains(&item.id)). At cycle 0 the two correction items retire each other (mutual retirement, ids C1/C3). At cycle 3 the newly captured 'always run the reporting suite ...' item is numbered C3, collides with the carried retired set, and is deleted with no supersession record. Rendered ledger of w9-a-late3c shows C2, C4, C5, C6 with C3 missing; w9-b-8c taskState loses q-constraint-3 the same way."
  },
  {
   "id": "W9-F3",
   "title": "baseline anchors overstate current-state retention: mutual retirement demotes constraints to history",
   "evidence": "In the baselines the 'never drop ...' and 'always run ...' correction-clauses retire each other; both anchors survive only inside SUPERSEDED (history) render lines, not as ACTIVE CONSTRAINTS. Anchor presence counts them as retained."
  }
 ],
